    "done_when": "Make the cold-compile ratio rows of the hand-written tangents insensitive to machine load. The shared harness tests/biot/tangent_identity.py judges tangent over primal compile on the median of seven fresh cache-disabled processes by wall time. Runs with modules in parallel saw tests/biot/test_elliptic_tangent_recurrences.py::test_tangent_compiles_within_three_primals[complete_pole], tests/biot/test_range_tangent_recurrences.py::test_tangent_compiles_within_three_primals[product] and the factorise row cross 3.0 only under concurrent load, and tests/biot/test_vertex_edge_tangent_recurrences.py::test_tangent_compiles_within_three_primals[graded_residual] fails in both arms even run sequentially: wall time measures the load as well as the compile. Done when: (1) the harness measures each fresh compile process by the child's CPU seconds (user plus system, from os.wait4 around exactly that child or resource.getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN) deltas) beside its wall, asserts the ratio on the median CPU seconds, and records both with the expanded equation counts; the 3.0 bound and the median of seven are unchanged and no absolute floor is added; (2) every compile row of the tangent modules (test_elliptic_tangent_recurrences.py, test_range_tangent_recurrences.py, test_vertex_edge_tangent_recurrences.py) passes twice sequentially and twice while eight concurrent CPU-bound processes run on the same allocation, all four runs logged in the run directory with each row's CPU and wall medians; graded_residual is diagnosed by name: if its tangent genuinely compiles above 3.0 times its primal by CPU seconds, say so with the equation counts and leave it red rather than loosening anything; (3) negative control: a tangent made deliberately heavier (its program evaluated twice and summed) fails its compile row under the CPU measurement, reported by pytest as a failing test id, ratio printed; (4) the identity, truncation and coverage rows are untouched and pass; tests/biot passe